# principles.topic -- recommended practices and the reasoning behind them.
# Some are backed by a warning (the page names it); most are not, because not
# every good practice can be detected precisely enough to warn about.

@topic principle.lint-precision
title: A warning must be worth investigating
summary: Botlish's warnings aim for precision over coverage: a legitimate exception to a rule is first evidence that the rule is too broad, not a reason to suppress it.
index: principles lint
alias: lint precision; false positive; overbroad warning; why is this warning wrong
keywords: precision, exception, suppress, silence, linter design
related: lint.policy lint.warnings-vs-errors principle.investigate-warnings warning.same-return-value warning.method-eligible
docs: WARNINGS-SAME-RETURN.md COMPILERPEDIA-REPORT.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
A warning makes a claim: this code is worth a second look. Because Botlish
has no per-warning switches and no suppression (lint.policy), every warning
is on for everyone, and a warning that habitually fires on reasonable code
teaches programmers to rewrite it mechanically until the warning stops --
which helps nobody. So a rule should claim only what the compiler can
establish, and missing some questionable code is better than flagging
reasonable code.

== IN PRACTICE
When a warning fires on code that is reasonable as written, that is
evidence about the rule. The compiler's maintainers narrow rules on such
evidence when a principled distinction can be made reliably -- and leave
them as they are, documenting the limit, when it cannot. Examples of the
former: SAME-RETURN-VALUE no longer counts the handlers of one call as
separate exits nor reports a guard-style predicate's repeated false;
METHOD-ELIGIBLE no longer suggests an Int literal as a receiver. Examples
of the latter: whether a function's first parameter is a natural receiver
is the author's judgment, declared with `nomethod`.

== WHEN IT DOES NOT APPLY
Errors are not lint: they are rules of the language, and precision there
means a clear diagnostic, not silence.

@topic principle.investigate-warnings
title: Investigate a warning; do not silence it mechanically
summary: Read what a warning claims and why; change the code when the claim points at a real problem, and keep it when the code is right.
index: principles lint
alias: respond to warnings; silence warning; fix warning
keywords: warning, investigate, rewrite, declaration
related: principle.lint-precision lint.policy

== THE PRINCIPLE
Each warning page (-help CODE) says what problem the rule is meant to
prevent. The useful response is to check whether the problem is there: a
SAME-RETURN-VALUE may reveal a condition that does not matter, or a sentinel
that should be a declared error; a METHOD-ELIGIBLE may reveal that a
function's first parameter is not a receiver and should be declared so.

== IN PRACTICE
  * The claim holds: change the code (merge the branches, name the parts,
    use a character literal, use method syntax).
  * The claim is about the function's interface: say it at the declaration
    (`nomethod fn`, a list result type, flags) -- each changes what the
    function is, not whether the warning prints.
  * The code is right and the rule cannot tell: keep the code. Restructuring
    correct code only to move the pattern out of the rule's sight hides the
    question instead of answering it; the evidence belongs in a report about
    the rule (principle.lint-precision).

@topic principle.name-the-parts
title: Name the parts of a multi-value result
summary: A function that returns several values returns a struct with named fields, which callers destructure, not a fixed-length list read by position.
index: principles
alias: name the parts; multi-value result; return several values; return two values; tuple
keywords: struct, result, destructure, positional, list
related: warning.fixed-arity-list-return concept.structs concept.destructuring rule.no-list-destructuring
docs: MULTI-VALUE-RESULTS.md STRUCT-DESTRUCTURING.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
`{field, next} = scan(text, i)` says what each part is at every use, is
checked by name, and survives a reordering. `r = scan(text, i)` followed by
`list::at(r, 0)` and `list::at(r, 1)` says nothing, must handle
IndexNotFound twice, and breaks silently when the order changes. Botlish
deliberately gives Lists no positional destructuring: a List is a
sequence.

== IN PRACTICE
Return an anonymous struct (`{field: f, next: i + 1}`) or a declared one,
and destructure it. Declare `-> List[T]` when the result really is a
sequence.

@example accept
fn scan(text: str, i: int):
    {field: text, next: i + 1}

{field, next} = "abc".scan(0)
next
@value 1
@quiet
@end

== CHECKED BY
FIXED-ARITY-LIST-RETURN (warning.fixed-arity-list-return), for a function
whose every exit is a list literal of one length.

@topic principle.receiver-first
title: Put the value an operation is about first
summary: Design functions so that their first parameter is the value they operate on, and call them with method syntax; declare `nomethod` when no parameter is a receiver.
index: principles
alias: receiver first; API design; method syntax style; receiver-oriented
keywords: method, receiver, API, nomethod, first parameter
related: concept.method-sugar concept.nomethod warning.method-eligible
docs: METHOD-SUGAR.md WARNINGS-METHOD-ELIGIBLE.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
Botlish's method syntax is exact sugar: `board.move(d)` is `move(board, d)`.
A function whose first parameter is its subject reads naturally as a
method, chains left to right, and finds its candidates through the
namespace the caller imports. A function whose first parameter is not a
subject (a name, a position, a count, a destination, symmetric operands)
reads badly as a method: `0.scan_while(p)`, `"rows".register(...)`.

== IN PRACTICE
Order parameters subject first; call with method syntax. For a function
with no receiver, write `nomethod fn`: method syntax becomes an error for
it, and METHOD-ELIGIBLE never suggests it.

== CHECKED BY
METHOD-ELIGIBLE (warning.method-eligible) reports functional calls the
method spelling would express, except where the compiler cannot judge the
receiver (an Int literal).

== WHEN IT DOES NOT APPLY
Constructors and other functions of no receiver (`solved()`,
`from_rows(rows)`), and symmetric operations (`bit_and(a, b)`).

@topic principle.options-are-flags
title: Options are flags
summary: A function's boolean options are flag parameters, named where they are used; positional true/false arguments are anonymous.
index: principles
alias: options are flags; boolean options; boolean parameters
keywords: flag, option, bool, argument
related: concept.flags warning.many-boolean-arguments rule.flags
docs: FLAGS.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
`open("f", :append)` says which option is on; `open("f", true, false)`
needs the declaration to be read, and two swapped literals are a silent
bug. A flag is false unless named at the call.

== CHECKED BY
MANY-BOOLEAN-ARGUMENTS (warning.many-boolean-arguments).

@topic principle.characters-not-strings
title: A character is a character, not a one-character String
summary: Write single characters as character literals ('x') and work with them through character operations (char_at, append_char, ==).
index: principles
alias: characters not strings; char vs string
keywords: UnicodeChar, character, literal, char_at, append_char
related: concept.strings warning.one-char-string-literal stdlib.str::char_at stdlib.str::append_char
docs: WARNINGS-ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
UnicodeChar is its own type: comparing `text.char_at(i) == ','` states a
character question exactly and allocates nothing; comparing one-character
substrings states it in String terms and allocates a String per position on
the native backends.

== CHECKED BY
ONE-CHAR-STRING-LITERAL (warning.one-char-string-literal), context-blind by
design: where an API takes a String for a character, the finding is about
the API.

@topic principle.errors-over-sentinels
title: Declared errors over sentinel values
summary: A function that can fail declares an error the caller must handle, instead of returning an in-band value (-1, "", []) that means "no result".
index: principles
alias: errors over sentinels; sentinel value; magic value; return -1
keywords: sentinel, error, fail, handle, optional, null
related: concept.errors warning.same-return-value warning.same-failure capability.null concept.error-payloads
docs: EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md ERROR-PAYLOADS.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
A sentinel is an ordinary value that the caller must remember to test, and
that may be a legitimate answer (-1 is an Int). A declared error is part of
the function's type: the compiler makes every caller handle it, admit it,
or prove it cannot happen, and a payload can say which failure occurred.
Botlish has no null and no optional type; declared errors are its failure
channel.

== IN PRACTICE
`fn find(xs, p) errors NotFound` rather than returning -1; `on NotFound:`
at the call. A function that genuinely has a default answer (`get(xs, i,
default)`) takes the default from its caller.

== WHEN IT DOES NOT APPLY
When the "empty" result is a real answer, not a failure: an empty List of
matches is a result.

@topic principle.guard-clauses
title: Guard clauses
summary: Dismiss the special cases first, each with its own early exit, so the main computation reads without nesting.
index: principles
alias: guard clauses; early return; early exit
keywords: guard, return, fail, nesting, precondition
related: warning.same-return-value warning.same-failure principle.errors-over-sentinels proof.list-bounds

== THE PRINCIPLE
`if xs == []: return 0` and `if i < 0 or i >= xs.length(): fail Invalid`
at the top of a function state its preconditions where they are checked
and keep the main path unindented. A guard is also how the compiler learns
facts: a guard on a parameter against a list's length proves later
`list::at` calls in range (proof.list-bounds).

== IN PRACTICE
One condition per guard when the reasons differ (each guard documents one
reason); one guard with `or` when they are one reason. Several guards that
answer the same non-Bool value report SAME-RETURN-VALUE: is it one result,
or a failure that should be named? A predicate's repeated false is not
reported.

@topic principle.restate-in-proven-form
title: Write what the compiler can prove
summary: When the compiler cannot prove an obligation away, restate the code in a form its analyses recognize, or make the outcome explicit -- never assume a check away.
index: principles proofs
alias: restate in proven form; unproven obligation; make it provable
keywords: proof, guard, handler, obligation, recognized form
related: proof.overview proof.list-bounds proof.slices proof.completions
docs: STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
A proof page lists the forms the compiler recognizes. Code in such a form
needs no handler for an error that cannot happen; code outside them must
say what happens if it does. Both are correct programs: the first is shorter
and states its invariant in a way the compiler checks.

== IN PRACTICE
Bind a field's list to a local name before a counted loop; guard a
parameter rather than a computed local (pass the computed index to a small
helper); iterate ascending, or iterate the elements. When the code is
clearer as it is, handle the error where it can occur, or use the
operation's explicit-default form (list::get).

@topic principle.bind-before-use
title: Bind compound values before using them as operands
summary: An `if` or a collecting loop produces a value only as the whole right side of `=` or the value of `return`: bind it to a name, then use the name.
index: principles
alias: bind before use; if as argument; loop as argument
keywords: if, loop, operand, argument, binding, helper
related: rule.if-placement rule.loop-placement concept.if-expressions concept.loops

== THE PRINCIPLE
Botlish's block-shaped expressions need their own lines, so they cannot sit
inside an operator or an argument list. Naming the value first is the
idiom, and it names what the value is.

== IN PRACTICE
    flags = loop b in boards:
        b.ok?()
    all_true?(flags)

    answer = if solved:
        "yes"
    else:
        "no"
    "status: ".concat(answer)

A small named helper (`fn yes_no(flag) -> str:`) serves when the choice
recurs.

@example accept
import str

fn status(solved: bool) -> str:
    answer = if solved:
        "yes"
    else:
        "no"
    "solved: ".concat(answer)

status(true)
@value "solved: yes"
@end

@topic principle.explicit-dependencies
title: Dependencies are explicit
summary: A file names what it uses (imports), a function names the environment it needs (context parameters), and nothing is reached through a global or by receiver type.
index: principles
alias: explicit dependencies; no globals
keywords: import, context, global, dependency, visibility
related: concept.imports concept.contexts rule.method-requires-import rule.imports-not-transitive
docs: IMPORTS.md CONTEXTS.md

== THE PRINCIPLE
Botlish has no global mutable state and no implicit imports: a namespace's
functions are visible only after `import NAME` (method syntax included), and
an execution environment such as the output channel is a context a
function declares and a program installs. What a file depends on is in its
header; what a function needs is in its signature.

@topic principle.predicate-naming
title: Name predicates with ?, validators with validate_
summary: A function answering a yes/no question ends in ?; a function that establishes a fact or fails is validate or validate_*; the name tells the reader a proof is made.
index: principles
alias: predicate naming; question mark names
keywords: predicate, validator, ?, naming, proves
related: warning.proves-naming concept.refinements
docs: REFINEMENT-VALUES.md

== CHECKED BY
PROVES-NAMING (warning.proves-naming), for functions with a `proves`
contract only.

@topic principle.immutability
title: Values do not change
summary: Bindings are immutable and values are immutable; a new value is computed instead of modifying an old one, except inside the explicit mutable collections.
index: principles concepts
alias: immutability; no mutation
keywords: immutable, value, mutation, MutableVector, MutableArray
related: concept.values concept.bindings capability.assignment concept.mutable-vector concept.mutable-array

== THE PRINCIPLE
`moved = board.move(d)` returns a new board; `board` is unchanged. A value
can be shared freely and reasoned about locally. Where in-place growth is
the point, MutableVector and MutableArray are mutable values with explicit,
checked places (concept.mutable-vector).

@topic principle.small-functions
title: Small named helpers
summary: Factor a recurring expression or a step that needs its own proof into a small named function: it gets a name, its parameters get guards, and the compiler proves it once.
index: principles
alias: small functions; helper functions
keywords: helper, function, refactor, guard, parameter
related: principle.restate-in-proven-form principle.bind-before-use proof.list-bounds

== THE PRINCIPLE
Several of the compiler's proofs work on parameters: an index guarded as a
parameter is related to a list's length, a computed local is not. A helper
that takes the computed value as a parameter both names the step and gives
the compiler the form it proves. Helpers are also how a value-producing
`if` is used as an operand (principle.bind-before-use).
